Q: How to set minimum and maximum characters limitation to EditText in Android? A: <pre><code>InputFilter[] lengthFilter = new InputFilter[1];
lengthFilter[0] = new InputFilter.LengthFilter(9); //Filter to 9 characters
mEditText.setFilters(lengthFilter);
</code></pre>

<p>This creates a filter to limit the number of characters.</p>

Q: Why does JSBin display a lint on “new function() { …&#160;}”? A: <p>If you're going to write an "anonymous type" equivalent to:</p>

<pre><code>var _anonymous_type = function () {
    ...
}

var t = new _anonymous_type();
</code></pre>

<p>You should:</p>

<pre><code>var t = new (function () {
    ...
})();
</code></pre>

<p>See the difference from you code? :)</p>

Q: Most efficient way to forward-fill NaN values in numpy array A: <p>My old solution for structured ndarrays. One can select any of the fields and pass their names as list form argument.</p>
<pre><code>def fillToTheEndSNA(sna, fields_names):
    for field_name in fields_names:
        match_rows_num_list = np.where(~np.isnan(sna[field_name]))[0]
        matches_count = match_rows_num_list.size
        if (matches_count &gt; 0):
            last =  match_rows_num_list[-1]
            sna[field_name][last + 1:] = sna[field_name][last] # fill the all row after last non-nan value

    return(sna)
</code></pre>

Q: Within an object literal, what does prefixing a property key with “get” do? A: <p>It's a <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/get" rel="nofollow">getter function</a>. It will look like a variable when you read it:</p>

<pre><code>var someService = nsPreferences.mPrefService;
</code></pre>

<p>It calls that function without using the regular invocation parens. You can also use the <code>set</code> operator to create a "setter" function for the same property:</p>

<pre><code>set mPrefService(val){
    this.actualVal = val;
},
</code></pre>



<pre><code>nsPreferences.mPrefService = "service";
</code></pre>
